The user is asking whether the recursive `render()` call bug in `resumeTransition()` was an actual bug in the yin GitHub repo, or if there was another way to solve the staggered monitor issue without modifying yin's code.

Let me think about this carefully.

The original bug in `resumeTransition()`:
```cpp
if (progress >= 1.0f) {
    m_transitionState = nullptr;
}
// This was outside the if block - ran every frame
if (m_wallpaper->m_isSingleFrame) {
    render();
}
```

This is definitely a bug. The `render()` call should only happen once after the transition completes to refresh the texture, but instead it's running every frame during the transition, causing an infinite recursive loop that collapses the entire animation into a single tick.

Could this have been solved differently? I could have avoided the issue by using a static transition type for static images instead of regular transitions, but that's not really solving the underlying problem. Another approach would've been detecting this at the shell level if I'd known the transition was being applied to a static image, but that would require more invasive changes to the architecture.

The core issue is that without this fix, transitions on static images snap instantly instead of animating smoothly. The comment clearly indicates this was meant to be a one-time post-transition cleanup, not something that runs every frame. So yes, this was a genuine bug in the yin repo—the only real workaround would've been to avoid using transitions with static images altogether, which defeats the purpose of having smooth animations.

